<% content_for :title, "About" %>
<% content_for :meta_description, "End Times is an interpretive Biblical news reader, not a prophecy decoder. We link out to original reporting and add original analysis." %>

<article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
  <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold">Methodology</p>
  <h1 class="font-display mt-2 text-4xl text-olive-deep">Interpretive, not a decoder</h1>

  <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-base leading-7 text-ink/90">
    <p>
      End Times gathers public headlines, links out to the original reporting, and writes a short Biblical reading beside them. We do not scrape or republish full article bodies. Licensing and honesty both point the same way: send readers to the publisher.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lens is broader than last-days speculation. Scripture talks about sin, bribes, famine, welcome, enemy-love, and a hope that outlasts empires. Prophecy is a first-class theme here, not the only frame. If a model reaches for Revelation on a thin pretext, the story is marked as a stretch so you can discount it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A local World English Bible (public domain) supplies the quotations. If a returned reference is missing from our index, we still show the citation and say the verse is not on hand.
    </p>
    <p>
      Selection is a mix of cheap AI and heuristics: connection quality, theme diversity, freshness, and a bias toward non-sensational sources. Editors can publish, hold, or reject anything. The machine will be wrong; that is why admin exists.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not pastoral counsel, not a claim that tonight’s news fulfills a secret calendar, and not a replacement for reading the Bible itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</article>
